Title: "Groups and Subgroups"
Id: "5bc745b6-657d-446f-b5f0-cae8c199112a"


Text: "A *group* is a monoid in which every element has an inverse: for each $x$
       there is a $y$ with $x * y = e$ and $y * x = e$. Groups are the central
       structure of elementary algebra, being exactly the structures whose
       operation can always be undone. They are the abstract theory of symmetry."
Id: "5fb80940-d8de-44bf-8117-eeee203d4755"


[\group]
Declares: G ::= (X, *, e) is \monoid via (X, *, e)
satisfies:
. forAll: x "in" G
  then:
  . exists: y "in" G
    suchThat:
    . x * y = e
    . y * x = e
Enables:
. capability: x_ "in" G :<->: x_ is \group.element:of{G}
  written: "x_? \in G?"
Documented:
. called: "group"
. description: "A group is a monoid in which every element $x$ has an inverse
                $y$, so that $x * y = e = y * x$. Equivalently it is an
                associative structure with identity in which every element is
                invertible; this makes it a quasigroup as well as a monoid."
Id: "e4d7371d-29bd-431f-ab09-e114fe89e5cf"


Text: "A group is *finite* when its underlying set has finitely many elements.
       The size of a finite group, its order, governs a great deal of its
       structure, and finiteness is inherited directly from the underlying
       monoid."
Id: "6c628805-5df1-4ad5-b2c3-23bf7dad5acc"


[\(finite)::group]
Refines: G
implicitly:
specifies: G is \(finite)::monoid
Documented:
. adjective: "finite"
Id: "28d2a1c1-89a5-4163-93e3-fbc21c97ec9a"


Text: "The elements of a group inherit the operation of the group, and each also
       carries an inverse. Writing $x^{-1}$ for the inverse of $x$ gives the
       familiar notation used in computations."
Id: "bb323028-2831-4d67-b784-b0918774ab3a"


[\group.element:of{G}]
Declares: x
when: G is \group
extends: x is \monoid.element:of{G}
Enables:
. capability: x.inv :=> \group.inverse:of{x}:in{G}
  written: "x+?^{-1}"
Documented:
. called: "element of group $G?$"
. description: "An element of a group is a member of its underlying set. Because
                the ambient structure is a group, each such element $x$ has a
                well-defined inverse $x^{-1}$, accessible through its inverse
                operation."
Id: "858abdda-c416-4a4e-a0bf-27ecf84cf008"


[\group.inverse:of{x}:in{G ::= (X, *, e)}]
Defines: y "in" G
when:
. G is \group
. x "in" G
expresses:
. x * y = e
. y * x = e
Documented:
. called: "group inverse of $x?$"
. written: "x+?^{-1}"
. description: "The inverse $x^{-1}$ of an element $x$ of a group is the unique
                element $y$ satisfying $x * y = e = y * x$. Its uniqueness is
                proved below; it is what allows every equation in a group to be
                solved."
Id: "9b52b844-0cfa-4238-8c73-8b38265cca81"


Text: "Two elements of a magma *commute* when the order in which they are
       combined does not affect the result. In a general group most pairs do not
       commute; the ones that do are of particular interest."
Id: "a36041ed-5bdd-4e9c-84ce-719a69b7708b"


[x \.commutes.with:?in{M ::= (X, *)}./ y]
States:
when:
. M is \magma
. x, y "in" M
that: x * y = y * x
Documented:
. called: "$x?$ commutes with $y?$@[M]{ in $M?$}"
. description: "Two elements $x$ and $y$ of a magma commute when $x * y = y *
                x$. An operation in which every pair of elements commutes is
                exactly a commutative one."
Id: "3d7a204c-966d-47e7-a5b4-8f9e20fd749c"


SectionTitle: "Subgroups"
Id: "29c1456f-88a5-4b56-a5b8-57859c40aedc"


Text: "Before defining subgroups we record a small set-theoretic fact that the
       definition needs: forming the Cartesian square of a subset lands inside
       the Cartesian square of the whole set. It lets a subgroup's operation be
       recognized as a restriction of the parent operation."
Id: "7541e6eb-f20a-497b-ae90-65c891609d26"


[\cross.of.subset.is.subset.of.cross]
Theorem:
given:
. X is \set
. Y \:subset:/ X
then:
. (.Y \.set.cross./ Y.) \:subset?:/ (.X \.set.cross./ X.)
Id: "db0160ae-5d28-4ad1-b2b6-d22964a88a2b"


Text: "A *subgroup* is a subset of a group that is itself a group under the
       inherited operation. It must contain the identity, be closed under the
       operation, and contain the inverse of each of its elements. Subgroups are
       the substructures through which the internal architecture of a group is
       studied."
Id: "91933534-107f-44fe-9ad6-4829664f8cd6"


[H ::= (X', *', e') \:subgroup:/ G ::= (X, *, e)]
Declares: H ::= (X', *', e')
when: G is \group
specifies:
. X' \:(nonempty)::subset:/ X
. *' is \binary.operation:on{X'}
. e' "in" X'
satisfies:
. (.*' := \restriction:of{`*`}:on{X' \.set.cross./ X'}.)[:1:]
. have:
  . forAll: x, y "in" H
    then:
    . x *' y "in" H
    . x *' y = x * y
  . forAll: x "in" H
    then: x.inv "in" H
  asserting: H is \group
Documented:
. called: "$H?$ is a subgroup of $G?$"
. written: "H? \leq G?"
. description: "A subgroup $H \leq G$ is a nonempty subset of $G$ that is closed
                under the group operation and under taking inverses, and whose
                operation is the restriction of that of $G$. Under these
                conditions $H$ is again a group in its own right."
Justification:
. [1]
  have: *' := \restriction:of{`*`}:on{X' \.set.cross./ X'}
  asserting: (.X' \.set.cross./ X'.) \:subset?:/ (.G \.set.cross./ G.)
  because: X' \:subset?:/ X
  by: \cross.of.subset.is.subset.of.cross#given{X := X; Y := X'}
Id: "d8fe82d3-6f50-4711-ad64-76d4eb0615ff"


Text: "That a subgroup is genuinely a group is recorded as a theorem, making
       explicit the property the definition was arranged to secure."
Id: "5a7d1bd6-3a8d-4584-bc3d-505eb0ba0680"


[\subgroup.is.group]
Theorem:
given:
. G is \group
. H \:subgroup:/ G
then: H is? \group
Id: "946d3180-8614-4bd2-8fd6-d9bdce721e04"


SectionTitle: "Abelian Groups and Inversion Theorems"
Id: "4f086ee9-6118-4df5-903c-f2463f65c0f1"


Text: "When the operation of a group *is* commutative, the group is called
       *abelian*. Abelian groups are far more tractable than groups in general,
       and they are the setting for much of number theory and linear algebra."
Id: "a608ecec-59d4-4939-a43e-3296c77eff39"


[\(abelian)::group]
Refines: G ::= (X, *, e)
satisfies:
. forAll: x, y "in" G
  then: x * y = y * x
Documented:
. adjective: "abelian"
Id: "1dc777cd-31a5-489a-8b5a-05b94c647fb9"


Text: "A first computation in the group axioms: the inverse of a product is the
       product of the inverses taken in the reverse order. The reversal is
       essential, since the operation need not be commutative."
Id: "a71309b4-a9ab-464d-a885-34cbcf992013"


Theorem:
given:
. G is \group
. x, y "in" G
then: (x * y).inv = y.inv * x.inv
Documented:
. called: "Inverse of a Product"
. description: "In any group, $(x * y)^{-1} = y^{-1} * x^{-1}$."
Id: "ef1b2ca8-1e12-4779-8e32-ed5cf8787c68"


Text: "Inverting an element twice returns the original element."
Id: "6619e809-8d59-4180-820d-ff40b5914fdf"


Theorem:
given:
. G ::= (X, *, e) is \group
. x "in" G
then: (x.inv).inv = x
Documented:
. called: "Double Inversion Law"
. description: "$(x^{-1})^{-1} = x$ for every element in a group."
Id: "8c61fb44-d5ab-4be8-804a-0d6a95d83331"


Text: "Any two elements that both act as an inverse of $x$ must be equal.
       Uniqueness is what justifies writing $x^{-1}$ for *the* inverse."
Id: "de8e5629-d42e-4d98-b713-62d92e61d306"


Theorem:
given:
. G ::= (X, *, e) is \group
. x, y1, y2 "in" G
where:
. x * y1 = e
. y1 * x = e
. x * y2 = e
. y2 * x = e
then: y1 = y2
Documented:
. called: "Uniqueness of Inverses"
. description: "If $y_1$ and $y_2$ both act as inverses for $x$, then
                $y_1 = y_2$."
Id: "a27fd487-85b4-4f18-b0ab-4be63db69f43"


SectionTitle: "Structural Consequences"
Id: "191bffbb-05de-4543-95d1-5161df7a4c17"


Theorem:
given:
. G is \group
. a "in" G
then:
. \left.cancellable{a}:in{G}
. \right.cancellable{a}:in{G}
Documented:
. called: "Group Cancellation Laws"
. description: "Every group element is both left and right cancellable."
Id: "24b48a0a-ec3e-49f2-acd4-80b979fcd209"


TextTheorem: "Lagrange's theorem. If $G$ is finite and $H\leq G$, then $|H|$
              divides $|G|$ and $|G|=[G:H]|H|$, where $[G:H]$ is the number of
              left cosets of $H$. In particular, the order of every element
              divides $|G|$. The converse need not hold: a divisor of $|G|$ need
              not be the order of a subgroup."
Id: "08d58b28-0472-45b2-b9f0-49c441c56fd8"


TextTheorem: "Cayley's theorem. Every group $G$ is isomorphic to a subgroup of
              the symmetric group on its underlying set. The embedding sends $g$
              to the bijection $x\mapsto gx$. Thus abstract groups are precisely
              groups of permutations, up to isomorphism."
Id: "d9f52cc6-df8a-4e42-8fa7-d06aeb3669db"
